//
// Expected: c0a1e1 at two sessions, c0a0e1 at one. Read the one-session
// verdict for the argument this model exists to make; at two sessions the
// authentication query additionally fails to a plain replay of Alice's own
// (salt#2, sealed#2), which forges nothing and is a separate finding from the
// desynchronisation below.
//
// The salt is the one HKDF argument that usually travels in
// the clear, and it is enough to desynchronise a key agreement. The attacker
// cannot read anything (ikm never leaves either principal) and cannot forge
// anything (Bob's AEAD_DEC halts him on the substituted key), but it CAN make
// the two sides derive different keys, and the equivalence query is where that
// shows up.
//
// The three verdicts together are the point: a denial of agreement is neither
// a confidentiality break nor an authentication break, and an engine that
// reported it as either would be reporting a false attack. Compare
// concat_bomb_equiv.vp, where halting the recipient is deliberately NOT a
// divergence — here Bob really does compute a key, and it really is the wrong
// one.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private ikm
	knows public info
	knows private m
	generates salt
	k_alice, _, _ = HKDF(salt, ikm, info)
	generates n_sealed
	sealed = AEAD_ENC(k_alice, n_sealed, m, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: salt, n_sealed, sealed

principal Bob[
	knows private ikm
	knows public info
	k_bob, _, _ = HKDF(salt, ikm, info)
	opened = AEAD_DEC(k_bob, n_sealed, sealed, nil)?
	_ = HASH(opened)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? m
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: sealed
	equivalence? k_alice, k_bob
]
